WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
Once a few genes slip out of the silence, how does just one of them win?

2
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:13.260
An enhancer is a stretch of DNA that raises transcription of a gene it contacts.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.800
The receptor genes have candidate enhancers, which the field calls the Greek

4
00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:19.800
Islands.

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.653
Contacts in trans are contacts between different chromosomes.

6
00:00:24.817 --> 00:00:28.737
A hub is a cluster of enhancers, often from different chromosomes.

7
00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:32.057
An active hub sits around the gene it drives.

8
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:38.520
Markenscoff-Papadimitriou and colleagues linked receptor transcription to contacts

9
00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.640
between enhancers on different chromosomes.

10
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.720
A chromatin remodeler, Bptf, helped make those contacts.

11
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.730
Disrupting the contacts was linked to weak expression of several receptors at once.

12
00:00:52.683 --> 00:00:53.823
What builds the hubs?

13
00:00:54.683 --> 00:01:01.283
Two transcription factors, Lhx2 and Ebf, bind cooperatively at enhancer motifs with

14
00:01:01.283 --> 00:01:02.283
a set spacing.

15
00:01:03.067 --> 00:01:08.707
Nearly every Ebf site in the receptor clusters was also bound by Lhx2.

16
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:14.507
An inserted array of enhancer elements raised how often linked receptors were

17
00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:17.407
chosen. That is a sufficiency-style test.

18
00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:23.707
Monahan, Horta and Lomvardas then extended the contact maps across the whole genome.

19
00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:28.850
Receptor clusters on 18 chromosomes contacted one another.

20
00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:35.890
The factors LHX2 and LDB1 regulated how these hubs assemble and are maintained.

21
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.800
But a contact map from many cells is an average.

22
00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.880
It cannot show that each cell has a single hub.

23
00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:46.130
So look inside one developing neuron.

24
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.450
Is there one hub, or several?

25
00:01:54.533 --> 00:01:56.553
Several, before one gene wins.

26
00:01:57.493 --> 00:02:01.913
Pourmorady and colleagues inferred competing hubs in single developing neurons.

27
00:02:03.017 --> 00:02:08.097
They combined single-cell readouts of RNA and open chromatin with single-cell

28
00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:09.097
contact maps.

29
00:02:12.467 --> 00:02:14.807
Their evidence supported two further steps.

30
00:02:15.687 --> 00:02:20.307
First, receptor RNA suppressed transcription of competing receptors at a distance.

31
00:02:21.467 --> 00:02:25.527
The machinery behind that long-range suppression is still unidentified.

32
00:02:26.717 --> 00:02:31.477
Second, translation of the receptor then stabilized transcription at the winning

33
00:02:31.477 --> 00:02:32.477
hub.

34
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:37.547
So competition can work without receptor protein, and protein then helps stabilize

35
00:02:37.547 --> 00:02:41.147
the winner. One receptor per neuron is the endpoint.
